Relevant tables and columns for the task
• interest_metrics  
  – interest_id: joins to interest_map.id  
  – composition: the metric we must rank on  
  – month_year: the month (MM‑YYYY) we need to display  
  – (_month, _year can be handy, but month_year already has the right format)

• interest_map  
  – id: primary key; equals interest_metrics.interest_id  
  – interest_name: descriptive category to show in the result

Typical filter / join conditions
• interest_metrics.interest_id = interest_map.id  
• We need the row(s) where composition is the maximum for each interest_id (to get the “highest composition value across all months” for every interest category).  
• After that, order those maxima descending (top 10) or ascending (bottom 10).

Exploratory‑to‑final SQLs  
(The queries go from simple inspection toward the full answer. All of them are valid SQLite syntax. LIMIT 20 added where useful.)

```sql
-- 1. Show 20 distinct month‑year values that exist in interest_metrics
SELECT DISTINCT month_year
FROM interest_metrics
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect 20 distinct interest_id values
SELECT DISTINCT interest_id
FROM interest_metrics
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Peek at a sample join between the two tables to make sure the
--    interest names line up with the IDs
SELECT im.interest_id,
       mp.interest_name,
       im.composition,
       im.month_year
FROM interest_metrics AS im
JOIN interest_map      AS mp
  ON im.interest_id = mp.id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. How many monthly records are there for each interest_id?
SELECT interest_id,
       COUNT(*) AS month_count
FROM interest_metrics
GROUP BY interest_id
ORDER BY month_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. What is the maximum composition observed for each interest_id?
SELECT interest_id,
       MAX(composition) AS max_composition
FROM interest_metrics
GROUP BY interest_id
ORDER BY max_composition DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Retrieve, for every interest_id, the month_year in which
--    that maximum composition occurred (ties keep the first one SQLite meets)
WITH max_comp AS (
  SELECT interest_id,
         MAX(composition) AS max_comp
  FROM interest_metrics
  GROUP BY interest_id
)
SELECT m.interest_id,
       i.month_year,
       m.max_comp
FROM max_comp AS m
JOIN interest_metrics AS i
  ON i.interest_id = m.interest_id
 AND i.composition   = m.max_comp
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Add the interest name and sort by the highest composition
WITH max_comp AS (
  SELECT interest_id,
         MAX(composition) AS max_comp
  FROM interest_metrics
  GROUP BY interest_id
)
SELECT mp.interest_name,
       im.month_year,
       max_comp.max_comp AS composition_value
FROM max_comp
JOIN interest_metrics AS im
  ON im.interest_id = max_comp.interest_id
 AND im.composition = max_comp.max_comp
JOIN interest_map AS mp
  ON mp.id = max_comp.interest_id
ORDER BY composition_value DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same idea but ascending order (bottom performers)
WITH max_comp AS (
  SELECT interest_id,
         MAX(composition) AS max_comp
  FROM interest_metrics
  GROUP BY interest_id
)
SELECT mp.interest_name,
       im.month_year,
       max_comp.max_comp AS composition_value
FROM max_comp
JOIN interest_metrics AS im
  ON im.interest_id = max_comp.interest_id
 AND im.composition = max_comp.max_comp
JOIN interest_map AS mp
  ON mp.id = max_comp.interest_id
ORDER BY composition_value ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Combine TOP 10 and BOTTOM 10 with a simple UNION ALL,
--    tagging each row
WITH max_comp AS (
  SELECT interest_id,
         MAX(composition) AS max_comp
  FROM interest_metrics
  GROUP BY interest_id
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT mp.interest_name,
         im.month_year,
         max_comp.max_comp AS composition_value
  FROM max_comp
  JOIN interest_metrics AS im
    ON im.interest_id = max_comp.interest_id
   AND im.composition = max_comp.max_comp
  JOIN interest_map AS mp
    ON mp.id = max_comp.interest_id
)
SELECT 'TOP 10' AS bucket,
       *
FROM ranked
ORDER BY composition_value DESC
LIMIT 10
UNION ALL
SELECT 'BOTTOM 10' AS bucket,
       *
FROM ranked
ORDER BY composition_value ASC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: neatly list TOP 10 and BOTTOM 10 in one query,
--     keeping ordering within each bucket
WITH max_comp AS (
  -- find the highest composition for every interest category
  SELECT interest_id,
         MAX(composition) AS max_comp
  FROM interest_metrics
  GROUP BY interest_id
),
best_rows AS (
  -- pull the month that carries that max value
  SELECT im.interest_id,
         im.month_year,
         mc.max_comp AS composition_value
  FROM max_comp AS mc
  JOIN interest_metrics AS im
    ON im.interest_id = mc.interest_id
   AND im.composition = mc.max_comp
),
ranked AS (
  -- add the human‑readable interest name
  SELECT mp.interest_name,
         br.month_year,
         br.composition_value
  FROM best_rows AS br
  JOIN interest_map AS mp
    ON mp.id = br.interest_id
)
SELECT 'TOP 10' AS bucket,
       interest_name,
       month_year,
       composition_value
FROM ranked
ORDER BY composition_value DESC
LIMIT 10

UNION ALL

SELECT 'BOTTOM 10' AS bucket,
       interest_name,
       month_year,
       composition_value
FROM ranked
ORDER BY composition_value ASC
LIMIT 10;
```

The tenth query can be run directly to produce the required list of the 10 highest‑ranking and 10 lowest‑ranking interest categories, each accompanied by the month (MM‑YYYY) when that category achieved its peak composition and the composition value itself.
